  "final_message": "I added all three views to `notebooks/explore.py`. The notebook passes `marimo check` and runs end to end. Each view is a chart with its table underneath. I edited the file directly rather than through a live marimo-pair session, so reopen or reload the notebook to see them.\n\n**Defining the dip:** the data covers 2023\u20132024, and the drop is in Q2 2024: 115 shipments, against 156 in Q1 2024 and 159 in Q2 2023. Each view compares Q2 2024 with both of those quarters. The charts show the change from Q2 2023, which removes normal seasonal swings. Q1 2024 is in the tables and tooltips. \"Shipments\" means the number of rows; I can switch to TEU or USD value if you'd prefer.\n\n**1. Total shipments per month:** a line chart over the 24 months. The April\u2013June 2024 low (35, 41 and 39) stands out clearly.\n\n**2. Dip by port:** three ports account for almost all of it.\n| Port | Q2 2023 | Q2 2024 | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fennick | 20 | 4 | \u221280% |\n| Brackwater | 21 | 7 | \u221267% |\n| Ivelholm | 16 | 4 | \u221275% |\n\nTogether they lost 42 of the 44 shipments that disappeared year over year. The other ports were roughly flat, and Dovesend and Juniper Reach went up.\n\n**3. Dip by carrier type:** all three types fell. Container lost the most shipments (92 \u2192 66, \u221228%). Tanker fell furthest in percentage terms (23 \u2192 13, \u221243%), though from a small base. Bulk went from 44 to 36 (\u221218%).\n\nBecause every carrier type fell while the drop sits in three specific ports, the cause looks port-specific rather than tied to carrier type. The next step would be to break those three ports down by carrier type.",
